Why do all my buttons alert 3?

```js
for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  const b = document.createElement('button');
  b.onclick = () => alert(i);
  document.body.append(b);
}
```

text:
Because `var i` is function-scoped (or global here), not block-scoped. There's only **one** `i` shared by all three click handlers. The loop finishes with `i === 3` (the value that made the condition `i < 3` false), and the handlers don't read `i` until you click, so each one sees 3.

## Fix: use `let`

```js
for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  const b = document.createElement('button');
  b.onclick = () => alert(i);
  document.body.append(b);
}
```

`let` in a `for` loop header creates a **fresh binding for each iteration**, so each arrow function closes over its own `i` (0, 1, 2).

## Alternatives

- **IIFE (old-school):**
  ```js
  for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
    (function (j) {
      b.onclick = () => alert(j);
    })(i);
  }
  ```
- **Capture in a block-scoped const:**
  ```js
  for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
    const n = i;
    b.onclick = () => alert(n);
  }
  ```
- **`forEach`/`Array.from`**, where the callback parameter is its own scope per call.

Using `let`/`const` and avoiding `var` is the simplest way to sidestep this class of bug.
